WHATNOW

What tam-clam is doing now and what comes next. Read this first; revise
it at the end of every session (work/README-work).

Updated: 2026-11-24, with W23 (0.3.3)


STATE

  Version 0.3.3, Phase P4 in progress (Oils-named strict modes), on
  branch dev. 0.3.3 adds:
  - strict_errexit (in src/exec.c): refuses running shell functions,
    compound commands (other than [[ ]]), or command sequences while
    errexit is ignored (if/while/until conditions, &&/|| LHS, !);
  - command_sub_errexit (in src/expand.c, src/exec.c, src/bi_declare.c,
    src/cond.c): failing $(...) or `...` aborts the enclosing command
    with its non-zero exit status (TC_EXP_CMDSUB_FAIL) instead of being
    swallowed by echo/local/etc.;
  - simple_word_eval (in src/expand.c): unquoted $x, $(...), $((...))
    do not undergo implicit IFS splitting or pathname globbing, while
    literal globs and ${a[@]}/$@ continue to work normally;
  - strict_argv (in src/exec.c): commands whose words expand to 0 args
    or an empty command name ("") fail with a clear error;
  - set -o strict / set +o strict (in src/options.c): single switch
    enabling/disabling errexit, nounset, pipefail, strict_errexit,
    command_sub_errexit, simple_word_eval, and strict_argv (all off by
    default so POSIX scripts are unchanged);
  - tests/test_strict.c (37 checks), tests/spec/strict.test.sh (8 cases),
    and sandbox/demo-0.3.3-strict.
  readelf -d lists only libc.so.6. make test, make sanitize, and make
  fuzz pass.

  Fred's decisions so far are in ROADMAP.md and the plans: POSIX.1-2024
  is the target; the Tamlinux-wide keybinding standard lives in
  tamlinux/arch/keybindings.md and tam-clam implements it in
  arch/keys.md (no vi mode, no built-in readline map, VINTR=Ctrl+\,
  VSUSP=Ctrl+P, Ctrl+F history search, Ctrl+Q quit); FUNCNEST sets the
  function recursion limit; signed 64-bit arithmetic overflow is a
  reported error; echo recognizes -n only and does not interpret
  backslash escapes by default; ** recursive globbing is off by default;
  strict modes are opt-in and off by default.


